  "critic_analysis": "The accepted baseline Harness (simple_search prompt, search tool, result_summary_prompt hook) achieves 80% accuracy on this 20-case HotpotQA experience set, with 4 failures and 0 retriever errors or runner errors. All 4 failures share a single underlying behavioral pattern: the model stops searching after 1–2 broad queries, retrieves partially relevant passages, and commits to an answer without decomposing the question into targeted sub-queries for the still-missing fact.\n\nCase evidence:\n- Case 2 (Guitars for Wounded Warriors / Ulster County): Model retrieves that the album was recorded in New Paltz, then finds 'Sullivan County' in an unrelated Woodstock passage in the same result batch. Instead of searching for 'New Paltz county', it guesses Sullivan County.\n- Case 3 (Just the Two of Us / 9 weeks): Model identifies the target song 'Bette Davis Eyes' via the Weiss/DeShannon search, but never issues a focused query for 'Bette Davis Eyes weeks at number 1'. Instead it plucks '5 weeks' from an unrelated 'The First Night' passage.\n- Case 4 (The Great Outdoors / Annette Bening / 2006): Model correctly identifies Annette Bening as the four-time nominee, then never searches for 'Annette Bening Hollywood Walk of Fame year'. It guesses 2015 from model knowledge.\n- Case 1 (Catching Fire / 16-year-old): Model retrieves a passage stating Katniss is 17-18 during the Quarter Quell. It accepts this as the answer without verifying which age range applies specifically to the novel Catching Fire vs. the broader series timeline. A targeted search for 'Katniss age Catching Fire novel' could have disambiguated.\n\nThe result_summary_prompt hook injects 'The available information may be insufficient. Please expand the search scope and increase the recall quantity to make a more accurate determination.' after every tool result. In cases 2, 3, and 4, the model receives this instruction and still fails to issue a follow-up query for the missing fact. In cases 3 and 4 (which used 2 searches each), the second query was also too generic or poorly targeted.\n\nThe failures are not caused by retriever outages (0 retriever errors), unsupported evidence (all questions are bridge-type with 'supported' filter status and high confidence), model knowledge limits (the model demonstrates correct entity identification in every case), or isolated sampling mistakes (the pattern crosses all 4 failures). This is a systematic failure to iteratively refine queries toward the precise missing fact after partial evidence is found.",
  "direction_index": 0,
  "problem_direction": {
    "problem": "The model stops searching after 1–2 broad queries and commits to an answer based on partial or incidental evidence, failing to decompose the question into targeted sub-queries for the specific missing fact.",
    "observed_pattern": "3 of the 4 failures involve the model correctly identifying an entity (New Paltz, Bette Davis Eyes, Annette Bening) but never issuing a focused follow-up query combining that entity with the unanswered attribute (county, weeks at #1, Walk of Fame year). The 4th failure (Catching Fire) shows the same pattern: the model retrieves a range (17-18) but does not search to verify which specific value applies. In all cases, the hook's instruction to expand search is present but the model either ignores it or responds with a second query that is still too broad.",
    "excluded_causes": [
      "Retriever outage or high retriever error rate (0 retriever errors across all 20 cases).",
      "Unsupported evidence (all 4 failing cases have 'supported' filter status with high confidence).",
      "Model knowledge limits (the model correctly identifies entities and relationships in every failure).",
      "Isolated sampling mistake (the pattern repeats across all 4 failures)."
    ],
    "desired_behavior": "After identifying a key entity from initial search results, the model should issue a dedicated follow-up query that combines that entity with the still-unanswered attribute from the question. For example, after finding 'New Paltz' ask 'New Paltz county New York'; after finding 'Bette Davis Eyes' ask 'Bette Davis Eyes weeks at number one Billboard'; after finding 'Annette Bening' ask 'Annette Bening Hollywood Walk of Fame year'.",
    "success_criteria": [
      "The model makes at least one search after the partial-entity identification that directly targets the missing numeric or categorical fact (county, year, count of weeks).",
      "The hook's prompt to expand search drives a more precise query rather than a similarly broad one.",
      "Accuracy on bridge-type questions requiring a concrete numeric or geographical attribute improves measurably."
    ],
    "constraints": [
      "Must not reduce accuracy on questions that are already answered correctly (the current 16 correct cases may already handle single-hop queries well).",
      "Must not increase tool call count excessively (current mean is 1.3 tool calls per case; adding 1-2 targeted follow-ups should be sufficient)."
    ]
  },
  "coordinator_analysis": "The post_tool hook intervention—instructing the model to issue a targeted follow-up query combining the identified entity with the unanswered attribute—was tested on all 4 failed cases. 3 of 4 succeeded (static pass or teacher score 1), correcting answers from wrong to correct. The single failure (Catching Fire) was caused by the instruction demanding 'exact number, not a range' when only a range existed, causing the model to fabricate a single value. The underlying search-redirect mechanism worked in all cases: each branch issued the targeted entity+attribute query as instructed. Execution cost remained comparable (2-3 steps, 1-2 tool calls) across successful cases. The pattern is validated across geographic, chart-statistic, and entertainment-year attribute types. Limitation: range-valued answers require instruction phrasing that doesn't force a single number. Further validation on more failure patterns would strengthen generalizability, but 3/4 distinct cases with different attribute types provide sufficient cross-case evidence.",
